D) this was a knowledge bottleneck... there were qite a few issues
- firstoy, my first issue was that I'd have a checker to determine if there exists a
  solution for k days apart which woudl involve calling std::qsort() and then using a for
  loop to greedily take the first items from the sorted array so its "a bit faster" but
  actually it ended up n^2...
- fixed this by realising instedad of calling a for loop repeatedly you can do this
  mathematically by
    1) precomputing the cycles: for every k day gap, this means that we pick the top [k]
    items (from the sorted quest coin array). This would mean that precomputing all cycles
    up to `num_days` is O(n), and the calling of this is O(1)
    2) instead of using a for loop to determine how many cycles we need, we would instead
    need to take the function checker's k days and do rycles[k+] * num_days/(k + 1) + the
    remainder which is cycles[num_days % k]
- turns out qsort is also slowing it down... becuase i used a lambda function to cmp.
  Turns out calling std::sort() is the smart solution and reimplmenting qsort from scratch
  is stupid :(
